            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "5 ft Cutie"
            },
            "reviewBody": "This was very yummy. Somenone blessed us with sliced ham. I threw everything in a sauce pan, cut up the ham slices and tossed everything together. Served it on brown and serve buns. I did end up adding 3tb orange juice instead of two on the count of my honey. I use a very strong honey. Over all, this is a keeper. Thank you !"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "5"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "mbournazian"
            },
            "reviewBody": "This is one of the best Hams that I have ever made! I actually doubled the recipe so I had plenty of glaze on hand. I made my ham the day before so I wanted to be able to rebaste it for reheating the next day. Came out wonderful and I'm so glad that I tried this. This is now going to be my stock ham recipe! My family loved it and I couldn't keep my husband away from it! Thanks for sharing.",
            "datePublished": "2010-11-29T09:05:22.730Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "5"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "Upallnite1"
            },
            "reviewBody": "It's the best ham glaze ever. Follow the recipe plus slightly adding more ingredients on all plus 1 tsp. brown sugar and 5 tbs. oj instead of 2 tbs. Follow the cooking method of the ham you have and the last baking time (glazing) for only 20 minutes so it won't dry. Cover loosely with foil and let stand outside the oven for 30 minutes. Make sure the ham is slit across before baking to let the fat drip and the glaze to penetrate. Best sliced and eaten when chilled overnight.",
            "datePublished": "2024-04-01T03:50:20.605Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "5"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "TJD912"
            },
            "reviewBody": "This is a really good recipe with understated sweetness. I use a bone-in ham and the un-used glaze is served on the side.",
            "datePublished": "2011-04-20T12:00:00.487Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "5"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "Joe Berkevich"
            },
            "reviewBody": "I did make some changes. the recipe itself no but while cooking the ham. I made it in a nesco roaster and sliced 2 oranges into medium to thick slices and laid them on the bottom. then i put my ham in the roaster and poured about a half a cup of ginger ale over top of the ham and let it cook for an hour. After that is when I basted with the glaze every 10 to 15 minutes, for about another 45 minutes. After about 2 hours I unplugged the nesco and took the ham out to cool, since i was using my ham to make sandwiches with for our Christmas dinner. It turned out really delicious and I plan to continue using this method for the next several years until i need a change in flavor.",
            "datePublished": "2018-12-22T04:38:30.317Z"
          },
          {
